INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('29768c0d-0309-555d-9f62-b68deebb0333', 'China Provisional Regulations on Carbon Emissions Trading (碳排放权交易管理暂行条例)', 'Provisional Regulations on Carbon Emissions Trading (碳排放权交易管理暂行条例)', 'The Provisional Regulations on Carbon Emissions Trading were passed by the State Council of China on January 5, 2024, and will take effect on May 1, 2024. This regulation establishes a nationwide carbon trading system, setting rules for emissions trading, compliance mechanisms, and government oversight.

Key aspects of the policy include:

Scope and Coverage: The regulation applies to carbon emissions trading activities nationwide, covering key greenhouse gas (GHG) emitting sectors.
Government Oversight: The Ministry of Ecology and Environment (MEE) is responsible for supervising and managing the carbon trading system, with provincial governments ensuring compliance at local levels.
Carbon Market Mechanisms:
Establishes national carbon emissions allowances (CEAs).
Allows for free and paid allocation of emissions permits, with a gradual shift to more paid allocations.
Regulates carbon offset mechanisms through Certified Emission Reductions (CERs).
Enables buying, selling, and trading of emissions permits within a unified national system.
Corporate Responsibilities:
Key emitting enterprises must report annual emissions data, ensure data accuracy, and comply with emissions quotas.
Companies must purchase additional allowances if their emissions exceed allocated quotas.
Enforcement & Penalties:
Non-compliance results in financial penalties (e.g., fines of ¥500,000 to ¥5 million for failing to meet reporting obligations or manipulating market data).
Fraudulent reporting, data manipulation, or failing to submit required emissions allowances may lead to criminal liability.
Market Supervision:
Carbon trading activities are subject to strict anti-manipulation rules to prevent fraud, collusion, or misinformation.
Government agencies will monitor trading transactions and emissions compliance through a unified digital platform.', 'The Provisional Regulations on Carbon Emissions Trading were passed by the State Council of China on January 5, 2024, and will take effect on May 1, 2024. This regulation establishes a nationwide carbon trading system, setting rules for emissions trading, compliance mechanisms, and government oversight.

