Corporate Responsibilities:
Key emitting enterprises must report annual emissions data, ensure data accuracy, and comply with emissions quotas.
Companies must purchase additional allowances if their emissions exceed allocated quotas.
Enforcement & Penalties:
Non-compliance results in financial penalties (e.g., fines of ¥500,000 to ¥5 million for failing to meet reporting obligations or manipulating market data).
Fraudulent reporting, data manipulation, or failing to submit required emissions allowances may lead to criminal liability.
Market Supervision:
Carbon trading activities are subject to strict anti-manipulation rules to prevent fraud, collusion, or misinformation.
Government agencies will monitor trading transactions and emissions compliance through a unified digital platform.', 'China', 'climate', 'in_force', '2024-01-01'::date, 'China', 'https://www.carrotsandsticks.org/policies/33_2', ARRAY['sdg13', 'regulations', 'sdg7', 'sdg11', 'sdg17', 'mandatory', 'china', 'disclosure', 'sdg9']::text[]) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('b868d371-cc8a-5868-82d9-ca324f0f1a60', 'China Shanghai Stock Exchange Self-Regulation Guidelines – Sustainability Reporting', 'Shanghai Stock Exchange Self-Regulation Guidelines No. 14 – Sustainability Reporting (Trial) (上海证券交易所上市公司自律监管指引第 14 号——可持续发展报告)', 'The Shanghai Stock Exchange (SSE) Guidelines on Sustainability Reporting provide a structured framework for listed companies in China to disclose environmental, social, and governance (ESG) information. These guidelines align with global best practices and require companies to integrate sustainability into their corporate strategy, operations, and governance.

Key aspects of the policy include:

Applicability:
Mandatory disclosure for companies in the SSE 180 Index, STAR Market 50 Index, and dual-listed firms.
Voluntary disclosure encouraged for other listed companies.
Disclosure Requirements:
Companies must publish a Sustainability Report (SR) or Environmental, Social, and Governance (ESG) Report within four months after the fiscal year-end.
Reports must cover financial materiality (impact on business operations) and impact materiality (effects on society and the environment).
Core Reporting Areas:
Governance: Oversight of sustainability risks and integration into corporate strategy.
Strategy: Management of sustainability risks and long-term business impacts.
Risk Management: Identification and monitoring of ESG risks and opportunities.
Metrics and Targets: Key indicators to measure progress in sustainability initiatives.
Key ESG Metrics:
Climate Change: Emissions disclosure, carbon reduction targets, adaptation strategies.
Environmental Protection: Pollution control, biodiversity protection, waste management.
Social Responsibility: Rural revitalization, community development, employee welfare.
Corporate Governance: Business ethics, anti-corruption, risk management.
Third-Party Assurance:
Encourages independent verification of sustainability reports for credibility.
Implementation Timeline:
May 1, 2024: Guidelines take effect.
2026: First mandatory sustainability reports for 2025 fiscal year.
2024 (Optional): Companies may voluntarily publish reports aligned with these guidelines.', 'The Shanghai Stock Exchange (SSE) Guidelines on Sustainability Reporting provide a structured framework for listed companies in China to disclose environmental, social, and governance (ESG) information. These guidelines align with global best practices and require companies to integrate sustainability into their corporate strategy, operations, and governance.

